WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.159 --> 00:00:02.579
(pigs squealing)

00:00:02.579 --> 00:00:03.742
- When I was in the coal industry,

00:00:03.742 --> 00:00:05.659
it was feast or famine.

00:00:07.291 --> 00:00:08.688
Everything's going great

00:00:08.688 --> 00:00:10.545
since they've shut down.

00:00:10.545 --> 00:00:11.646
I've got some eggs, man.

00:00:11.646 --> 00:00:13.383
Yeah, I went from working

00:00:13.383 --> 00:00:16.097
in a coal mine, blowing up
the mountains, and now I'm

00:00:16.097 --> 00:00:18.025
planting stuff on top of it, you know?

00:00:18.025 --> 00:00:19.244
Reclaiming it back.

00:00:19.244 --> 00:00:21.827
(violin music)

00:00:26.876 --> 00:00:29.376
(piano music)

00:00:32.857 --> 00:00:33.690
My name is Chris Farley,

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:36.313
and I'm a farmer for Refresh Appalachia.

00:00:36.313 --> 00:00:38.313
(sheep bleating)

00:00:38.313 --> 00:00:39.153
It's okay, buddy.

00:00:39.153 --> 00:00:41.733
Refresh Appalachia has
come to these abandoned

00:00:41.733 --> 00:00:43.615
surface mines and we're turning

00:00:43.615 --> 00:00:46.873
them into active agriculture, farms.

00:00:46.873 --> 00:00:49.541
This one will probably get
to be about five feet tall.

00:00:49.541 --> 00:00:50.873
Everything you see here

00:00:50.873 --> 00:00:52.575
was once an active coal mine.

00:00:52.575 --> 00:00:53.408
What are you doing buddy?

00:00:53.408 --> 00:00:54.552
Ay-yay-yay.

00:00:54.552 --> 00:00:55.577
- And the way I like to do it is

00:00:55.577 --> 00:00:57.297
just kinda step on it and wiggle it.

00:00:57.297 --> 00:00:58.657
- [Chris] And almost everybody that works

00:00:58.657 --> 00:00:59.956
for Refresh Appalachia was

00:00:59.956 --> 00:01:02.217
a laid-off coal miner.

00:01:02.217 --> 00:01:04.356
We have chickens that fertilize the soil.

00:01:04.356 --> 00:01:06.092
We have goats that eat the tops

00:01:06.092 --> 00:01:08.137
off the bushes.

00:01:08.137 --> 00:01:10.636
And we have the hogs that come
in and they're like a plow.

00:01:10.636 --> 00:01:13.186
They tear up all the rocks and the soil

00:01:13.186 --> 00:01:14.544
and get it ready for plantin'.

00:01:14.544 --> 00:01:17.377
(orchestra music)

00:01:18.944 --> 00:01:22.854
This soil is so tough,
we have to use pick axes,

00:01:22.854 --> 00:01:25.645
slate bars, just try to break it up.

00:01:25.645 --> 00:01:26.562
It's tough.

00:01:28.386 --> 00:01:29.925
Perfect.

00:01:29.925 --> 00:01:33.206
I started in the coal mines
right outta high school.

00:01:33.206 --> 00:01:35.145
(guitar music)

00:01:35.145 --> 00:01:37.654
I just followed in my father's footsteps.

00:01:37.654 --> 00:01:39.552
I used to work on the surface mines,

00:01:39.552 --> 00:01:42.313
for over 11 years, similar
to what you see back here.

00:01:42.313 --> 00:01:44.396
The first six years was good,

00:01:44.396 --> 00:01:45.861
and after that it was every six months

00:01:45.861 --> 00:01:47.793
to a year I'd get laid off.

00:01:47.793 --> 00:01:49.422
Times were real tough.

00:01:49.422 --> 00:01:50.480
Garden looks good.

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:52.412
How much rain have you had down here?

00:01:52.412 --> 00:01:53.982
My grandmother and grandfather

00:01:53.982 --> 00:01:55.044
had a farm their whole life.

00:01:55.044 --> 00:01:56.993
They went through the Great Depression.

00:01:56.993 --> 00:01:58.170
A picture of my grandpa.

00:01:58.170 --> 00:01:59.011
They didn't struggle at all,

00:01:59.011 --> 00:02:00.760
because they had their own farm.

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:02.810
Hey Gran, how was the
sweet potatoes this year?

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:05.641
- Last year they were really good.

00:02:05.641 --> 00:02:07.683
- My grandma, she's old
but she's taught me a lot

00:02:07.683 --> 00:02:10.883
about gardening, and that's
something I've growed [sic] up loving.

00:02:10.883 --> 00:02:13.192
Yeah, I started out farming,

00:02:13.192 --> 00:02:14.781
went into the coal mines,

00:02:14.781 --> 00:02:16.723
now I'm back to farming, back to my roots.

00:02:16.723 --> 00:02:18.483
We'll have this bunch come in first,

00:02:18.483 --> 00:02:21.352
and then this bunch will come in.

00:02:21.352 --> 00:02:23.701
If I had an offer to go back
into the coal mines right now,

00:02:23.701 --> 00:02:25.392
I definitely wouldn't take it.

00:02:25.392 --> 00:02:28.796
I'm hoping that we can have
the strip jobs workin',

00:02:28.796 --> 00:02:30.821
and we can come behind them, plantin',

00:02:30.821 --> 00:02:32.552
like we're doing right now.

00:02:32.552 --> 00:02:34.491
There's opportunity there.

00:02:34.491 --> 00:02:36.872
We can work together, and make it happen.

00:02:36.872 --> 00:02:39.455
(guitar music)

